1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,981
From the cross, in His darkest hour, Jesus

2
00:00:03,101 --> 00:00:06,243
reached for words already written, a psalm

3
00:00:06,303 --> 00:00:08,384
David had penned a thousand years before.

4
00:00:09,824 --> 00:00:12,485
Psalm twenty-two opens in the voice of a forsaken

5
00:00:12,545 --> 00:00:15,026
man; Jesus made it His own.

6
00:00:16,227 --> 00:00:20,709
David's very first line: "My God, my God, why

7
00:00:20,809 --> 00:00:23,810
hast thou forsaken me?" A thousand years later,

8
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:26,691
at the ninth hour, Jesus cried it from the

9
00:00:26,731 --> 00:00:31,233
cross: "My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken

10
00:00:31,293 --> 00:00:33,114
me?" The very same words.

11
00:00:34,455 --> 00:00:37,716
This was no cry of lost faith, even forsaken,

12
00:00:38,096 --> 00:00:42,658
He still calls God "my God." It was the sinless

13
00:00:42,698 --> 00:00:45,799
Son taking our place, and bearing the forsaking

14
00:00:45,839 --> 00:00:47,280
that our sin deserved.

15
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,122
He was forsaken so that you never will be.

16
00:00:52,262 --> 00:00:55,143
However far you've run, the way home is open

17
00:00:55,163 --> 00:00:58,084
and He opened it from the dark.
